  "body": "**Stacked on #1574** (#1548, head fd1a1c94): this branch starts from that head, so until #1574 merges the diff below also shows its commits. The commits that belong to this PR are 820529a3 and bda36c6e.\n\n## What changed\n- New `crates/app/src/geometry/import/step_closed.rs`. It finds the shells the file declares closed (`CLOSED_SHELL`, and the outer shell a `MANIFOLD_SOLID_BREP` names). It also counts unmatched edges: an edge not shared by exactly two facets running opposite ways, with vertices welded by neighbourhood within 1e-6 of the file's length unit, which is truck's `TOLERANCE`.\n- `step.rs::shell_facets` now records, for each tessellation pass, the first face truck returned no polygon for. `to_polygon` drops such a face without any error. On the pass that refinement kept:\n  - **Closed shell that meshes open: refused.** The message reads `closed shell #id (N faces) meshes open: K unmatched edges, the first from (..) to (..) mm; face #F (i of N) got no mesh from the kernel - ...`.\n  - **Face with no mesh: refused in any shell, open or closed.** The message reads `face #F (i of N) in shell #id could not be tessellated: the kernel ... returned no mesh for it - ...`.\n- An `OPEN_SHELL` or surface model may still mesh open. Only a dropped face is refused there.\n- Downstream checked: the default stock backend accepts an open mesh as a model. Only the SDF backend (`validate_shell`) refuses one. So the importer is the place to catch this.\n- Volume shortfall: not reported. The true volume of the B-rep is unknown, and the signed volume of an open mesh means nothing. The message carries the edge count and the first edge's position instead. The decision is recorded in `docs/decisions/1573-closed-shell-watertight.md`.\n\n## How tested\nNew suite `crates/app/tests/import_step_closed_shell.rs`:\n- `a_closed_shell_that_meshes_open_is_refused_by_name`: `fillet_corner.step` with its blend weights bent to `1E300, 1E-300`. Truck drops the blend. Before this PR the file imported open with no refusal.\n- `a_face_the_kernel_cannot_mesh_is_refused_in_an_open_shell_too`: the same bent file, declared `OPEN_SHELL` inside a `SHELL_BASED_SURFACE_MODEL`.\n- `every_solid_step_fixture_still_imports`: box, bspline_dome, fillet_corner, Milling-test, Milling-test-inch, sample_ap214, lathe_holder_offset and tool_csys_box_iso13399 all still import.\n- Unit tests in `step_closed`: a cube is closed, a cube missing one facet has 3 unmatched edges, and copies within the weld still meet.\n\n**Mutation check** (each refusal disabled in turn, then the suite rerun):\n- Closed-shell refusal disabled (`if edges > 0` changed to `if false && edges > 0`): `a_closed_shell_that_meshes_open_is_refused_by_name` FAILS. The import falls through to the dropped-face message.\n- Dropped-face refusal deleted: `a_face_the_kernel_cannot_mesh_is_refused_in_an_open_shell_too` FAILS. The file imports.\n\nGate: `cargo xtask gate quick --base upstream/main` is green on bda36c6e: app lib plus the 11 import suites (bore_facet_sagitta, import_step_*, large_models, model_fidelity, step_*, stock_export). `cargo clippy -p camcraft-app --all-targets -D warnings` is clean.\n\n## Not exercised\n- A closed shell that meshes open because a face was trimmed wrongly while every face still got a polygon. No fixture for that case is committed. The open-edge count covers it, and the first test shows the count fires on its own: with the dropped-face refusal as a fallback, deleting the closed-shell line changes the message.\n- Files with `BREP_WITH_VOIDS` or `ORIENTED_CLOSED_SHELL` voids: only `CLOSED_SHELL` and the `MANIFOLD_SOLID_BREP` outer shell are read as closed.\n- The release-lane heavy models.\n\nChangelog: STEP import refuses, by name, a closed shell whose mesh comes out open, and any face the kernel cannot mesh, instead of importing a model with walls missing.\nEffort: high (no escalation)\n\nfork synced at v0.19.0\n\nFixes #1573\n\n🤖 Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)\n",
